# The bundled IdP's only entrance (docs/plan/31_console-sso-rbac.md, #56).
#
# Two jobs, both enforced rather than advised:
#
#  1. Refuse Casdoor's SAML and CAS surfaces. VU#780781 concerns a cluster of
#     Casdoor CVEs, five of which live on the SAML SP path (/api/acs and its
#     login entry point). We use none of SAML or CAS — the console speaks OIDC
#     authorization-code + PKCE — so the safest configuration is for those
#     routes to not exist. Casdoor serves its API and UI from one port, so the
#     browser must be able to reach it; "internal-only networking" is therefore
#     not available as a control, and this proxy is.
#
#  2. Terminate TLS for the control plane. internal/identity/fetch.go requires
#     an https key-set URL and its dial guard then refuses loopback addresses
#     with no exception, so a plain-HTTP IdP cannot be wired to this platform at
#     all. The browser keeps plain HTTP on :8000 (which is also the issuer, and
#     so the `iss` claim); the control plane fetches keys from :8443 over a
#     certificate this proxy issues from its own local CA.

{
	# No admin API: nothing here is reconfigured at runtime, and it would be
	# another listener inside the stack.
	admin off
}

(saml_deny) {
	# Path-only, method-agnostic on purpose: Casdoor registers
	# /api/saml/redirect/:owner/:application for ANY method, so matching on the
	# method would leave a hole. The whole /cas/ tree goes because we use no part
	# of CAS, which is simpler to reason about than enumerating its validators.
	#
	# Deliberately NOT blocked: /login/oauth/* and /api/login/oauth/* — that is
	# the authorization-code flow this platform actually uses.
	@saml path /api/get-saml-login /api/acs /api/saml/metadata /api/saml/redirect/* /cas/*
	respond @saml "not found" 404
}

# The browser's entrance, and the issuer. Plain HTTP is correct here: this is
# what `origin` and IDENTITY_OIDC_ISSUER are set to, it is published on loopback
# only, and the accounts behind it are well-known dev credentials.
http://:8000 {
	import saml_deny
	reverse_proxy casdoor:8000
}

# The control plane's entrance, unpublished. `tls internal` issues from a local
# CA generated on first boot and stored in the idpca volume, which the control
# plane mounts read-only and trusts through SSL_CERT_FILE — so nothing private
# is committed and nothing expires on a schedule anyone has to remember.
https://idp:8443 {
	tls internal
	import saml_deny
	reverse_proxy casdoor:8000
}
